[48;2;223;239;233m[38;2;31;111;92m[1m Q1 [22m[38;2;36;31;27m You loved your daughter, Miriam, more than anything. You told yourself that—while tucking her in, while driving her to piano lessons, while framing her school photos in gold. You told everyone that—your coworkers, your sister, the priest at St. Anne’s who nodded when you said, “She’s my reason.” [0m

[48;2;247;227;220m[38;2;168;55;27m[1m Q2 [22m[38;2;36;31;27m You killed David, Miriam’s husband, not with a weapon, but with silence. When Miriam called you that winter night, voice trembling, saying David threatened her, you told her to stay calm, to not escalate. You said, “He loves you. He wouldn’t hurt you.” You called David after—friendly, concerned—asked him to fix the furnace at your place so he’d leave Miriam’s house. He never went back. [0m

[48;2;246;237;214m[38;2;138;106;18m[1m Q3 [22m[38;2;36;31;27m The lie you told was small: I was protecting the family. [0m

[48;2;222;233;244m[38;2;44;90;138m[1m Q4 [22m[38;2;36;31;27m You wanted people to see you as steady. The rock. The mother who held everyone together. [0m

[48;2;247;223;238m[38;2;147;48;107m[1m Q5 [22m[38;2;36;31;27m The secret is this: you kept David’s last text—*She’s lying, I’d never touch her*—and deleted it. Not because you believed him, but because you couldn’t bear Miriam to be someone who lied. And if she lied, then you had done nothing when she needed you most. [0m

